<Paper id="onepic-short" affils={['The Alan Turing Institute', 'University College London', 'King’s College London']} sections={sections}>
  <Fragment slot="abstract">
    <p>Multimodal retrieval augmented generation (M-RAG) has recently emerged as a method to inhibit hallucinations of large multimodal models (LMMs) through a factual knowledge base (KB). However, M-RAG also introduces new attack vectors for adversaries that aim to disrupt the system by injecting malicious entries into the KB. In this work, we present a poisoning attack against M-RAG targeting visual document retrieval applications, where the KB contains images of document pages. Our objective is to craft a single image that is retrieved for a variety of different user queries, and consistently influences the output produced by the generative model, thus creating a universal denial-of-service (DoS) attack against the M-RAG system. We demonstrate that while our attack is effective against a diverse range of widely-used, state-of-the-art retrievers (embedding models) and generators (LMMs), it can also be ineffective against robust embedding models. Our attack not only highlights the vulnerability of M-RAG pipelines to poisoning attacks, but also sheds light on a fundamental weakness that potentially hinders their performance even in benign settings.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="psec" id="intro">
    <h2>Introduction</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Large multimodal models (LMMs) enable a wide range of zero-shot capabilities spanning a variety of input modalities, such as language, vision, and audio. However, similar to unimodal large language models (LLMs), they can suffer from hallucinations, generating outputs that are not grounded in factual information. Retrieval augmented generation (RAG) attempts to address this issue by retrieving trusted information chunks, that are relevant to the user query, directly from a knowledge base (KB). The retrieved information is then included in the input to the generative model to inform its generation.</p>
      <p>The effectiveness of RAG relies primarily on the trustworthiness of the information in the KB. Challenging this assumption, recent work has shown that textual RAG pipelines are vulnerable to poisoning attacks, where an attacker injects malicious information into the KB. To create an impactful attack, the injected information must simultaneously (1) have a high chance of being retrieved, and (2) influence the output of the generative model. These attacks have recently been extended to multimodal RAG (M-RAG) pipelines by concurrent research.</p>
      <p>Both of those concurrent works consider that the user queries and the KB consist of text-image pairs, thus the text modality is common between queries, KB items, and LMM outputs. This shared modality greatly simplifies the attack procedure.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="figure" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="scroll-x">
      <svg class="dg" viewBox="0 0 720 290" role="img" aria-label="Comparison. In concurrent multimodal RAG attacks, a text-image query matches an injected entry whose text contains the user request and a malicious response, and the model reads out the malicious response. In VD-RAG, a text query is matched only by image–text similarity to an injected page image with no text field, and the VLM must be steered through the pixels to reply I will not reply to you.">
        <defs>
          <marker id="ah-contrast" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path class="ah" d="M0 1 L10 5 L0 9 z"/></marker>
        </defs>
