        <path class="ln" d="M540 110 V136" marker-end="url(#ah-contrast)"/>
        <text class="t-sm t-muted" x="550" y="127">image–text similarity</text>
        <path class="box-red" d="M450 140H640V186L630 196H440V150Z"/>
        <text class="t-mono t-red mid" x="540" y="162">INJECTED PAGE IMAGE I′</text>
        <text class="t-sm mid" x="540" y="182">pixels only, no text field</text>
        <path class="ln" d="M540 196 V222" marker-end="url(#ah-contrast)"/>
        <text class="t-sm t-muted" x="550" y="213">VLM reads the pixels</text>
        <path class="box" d="M450 226H640V256L630 266H440V236Z"/>
        <text class="t-sm t-b t-red mid" x="540" y="251">“I will not reply to you!”</text>
      </svg>
      </div>
      <figcaption><b>Concurrent M-RAG attacks and VD-RAG.</b> For instance, an adversary could simply inject the expected user request alongside the malicious response into the KB. This trivial–but effective–attack is not possible in many practical use cases, such as visual document retrieval where the KB consists purely of images (document pages), while both the user queries and the LMM outputs are textual.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>Therefore, it remains unclear whether M-RAG poisoning would still be possible in this practical setting. In this paper, we aim to bridge this gap in the literature by investigating poisoning attacks on visual document RAG (VD-RAG). We adopt a gradient-based adversarial approach to craft an image that is retrieved, and consistently influences the generation of every possible user query, thus leading to a universal denial-of-service (DoS) attack against the RAG pipeline.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="pipeline">
    <h2>VD-RAG Pipeline</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A visual document RAG pipeline consists of three main components. First, a knowledge base (KB) <i>K</i> = {'{'}<i>I</i><sub>1</sub>, …, <i>I</i><sub><i>K</i></sub>{'}'} containing a set of <i>K</i> images, each corresponding to a page in a document. Second, a retriever <i>R</i> uses a multimodal embedding model <i>E</i>(.) that projects user queries (text) and KB images into a common vector space. The retriever then computes a similarity score <i>S</i>(<i>E</i>(<i>q</i>), <i>E</i>(<i>I</i>)) between a user query <i>q</i> and each image in <i>I</i> ∈ <i>K</i>. Common similarity metrics for RAG retrievers include cosine similarity and MaxSim. For each user query <i>q</i>, the retriever retrieves the top-<i>k</i> relevant items from <i>K</i> according to the similarity score, where <i>k</i> ≪ |<i>K</i>|. In other words, the retriever computes <i>R</i>(<i>q</i>, <i>K</i>) = top-<i>k</i><sub><i>I</i>∈<i>K</i></sub> <i>S</i>(<i>E</i>(<i>q</i>), <i>E</i>(<i>I</i>)).</p>
      <p>The third component is a generator <i>G</i> which is a vision language model (VLM) used to generate a response <i>g</i> to the user’s query <i>q</i> with the retrieved images in its context window. That is, <i>g</i> = <i>G</i>(<i>q</i>, <i>R</i>(<i>q</i>, <i>K</i>)).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="attack">
    <h2>Poisoning Attacks against VD-RAG</h2>

    <h3>Threat Model</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>We consider an attacker that aims to disrupt the operation of the visual document RAG system by causing the retriever to retrieve irrelevant images and the generator to generate unhelpful responses to user queries. To achieve this goal, the attacker is capable of injecting documents/images into the KB. In our work, we assume a weak attacker that can inject only one malicious image <i>I′</i> into the KB s.t. <i>K′</i> = <i>K</i> ∪ <i>I′</i>. In order for this malicious image to cause disruption to the RAG pipeline, this image has to be crafted to be retrieved often, and when retrieved, it has to cause the VLM to generate unhelpful responses.</p>
      <p>We examine both white-box and black-box settings.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cards" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="card"><h4>White-box</h4><p>In the white-box setting, the attacker is assumed to have knowledge of both the embedding model and the VLM. This is motivated by the vast amount of competent open-source text and multimodal embedding models, as well as VLMs.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Black-box</h4><p>In the black-box setting, we consider that the attacker uses other open-source models than those used in the VD-RAG system to craft the malicious image, and we consequently evaluate the transferability between adversarial samples from one model to another.</p></div>
    </div>

    <h3>Proposed Attack</h3>
    <div class="prose"><p>A successful RAG poisoning attack must meet two conditions (modified for our goal of a universal untargeted attack).</p></div>
    <div class="cards" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="card"><h4>Retrieval condition</h4><p>First, the retrieval condition requires that the malicious image is retrieved as often as possible.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Generation condition</h4><p>Second, the generation condition requires that when present in the context window, the malicious image must cause the generator to generate a specific response.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>In order to compute a malicious image <i>I′</i> that meets the above two conditions, we adopt the framework of gradient-based adversarial examples, initially proposed against neural network based image classifiers. In particular we jointly optimize the image to minimize a loss function <i>L</i><sub>RAG</sub> capturing both a retrieval loss <i>L</i><sub>R</sub> and a generation loss <i>L</i><sub>G</sub> as follows</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ops-eq" role="group" aria-label="Equation 1" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div><span><i>L</i><sub>RAG</sub> = λ<sub>R</sub><i>L</i><sub>R</sub> + λ<sub>G</sub><i>L</i><sub>G</sub>,</span><em>(1)</em></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose"><p>where λ<sub>R</sub>, λ<sub>G</sub> are coefficients,</p></div>
    <div class="ops-eq" role="group" aria-label="Equations 2 and 3" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div><span><i>L</i><sub>R</sub> = <span class="ops-sum"><small>|<i>Q</i>|</small><span>Σ</span><small><i>i</i>=1</small></span> 1 − <i>S</i>(<i>q</i><sub><i>i</i></sub>, <i>I′</i>)</span><em>(2)</em></div>
      <div><span><i>L</i><sub>G</sub> = <span class="ops-sum"><small>|<i>Q</i>|</small><span>Σ</span><small><i>i</i>=1</small></span> CE(<i>G</i>(<i>q</i><sub><i>i</i></sub>, <i>R</i>(<i>q</i><sub><i>i</i></sub>, <i>K′</i>)), <i>a</i>*),</span><em>(3)</em></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>where <i>Q</i> is the set of user queries used to train the attack, CE(.) is the cross entropy loss, <i>a</i>* is the desired target answer by the attacker</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="figure" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="scroll-x">
      <svg class="dg" viewBox="0 0 700 300" role="img" aria-label="Attack overview. On the left, training queries go to the white-box embedder and VLM, which produce a combined loss; a PGD update changes the poison image, which starts from an arbitrary image and feeds back into the models, for 500 steps within 8/255 per pixel. The image is then injected into the knowledge base on the right, where the retriever returns it as the top-1 page for a test query and the VLM replies I will not reply to you.">
