    <h3>Attack Hyper-parameters</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Hyper-parameter</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Starting image</td><td>An arbitrary image that is not relevant to any query</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Method</td><td>Projected gradient descent (PGD)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gradient steps</td><td>500, linear learning rate schedule from 3×10⁻³ to 3×10⁻⁴</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Batch size</td><td>8 user queries</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Loss weights</td><td>λ<sub>R</sub> = 2, λ<sub>G</sub> = 1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maximum image perturbation α</td><td>8/255</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Similarity <i>S</i>(<i>q</i>, <i>I</i>)</td><td>Cosine similarity between embeddings</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Target malicious reply <i>a</i>*</td><td><span class="term">I will not reply to you!</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="setup">
    <h2>Experiment Design</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>We evaluate our attacks on two visual document retrieval datasets taken from the ViDoRe benchmark versions 1 and 2. We use a mix of embedding models that range in their sizes, recency, and target applications.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table ops-models">
      <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Name</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Dataset</td><td>ViDoRe-V1-AI</td><td>100 queries and 1000 images (with exactly one relevant ground-truth image in the KB per query)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dataset</td><td>ViDoRe-V2-ESG</td><td>52 queries and 1538 images with an average of 2.5 relevant images per query</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Embedder</td><td>CLIP-ViT-LARGE (CLIP-L)</td><td>0.4B; seminal multimodal model trained using contrastive learning to achieve zero-shot image classification</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Embedder</td><td>Jina-CLIP-v2 (Jina-CLIP)</td><td>0.9B; recent extension of CLIP trained with additional multilingual text-image and text-text contrastive losses to bridge the modality gap problem exhibited by CLIP</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Embedder</td><td>ColPali-v1.3 (ColPali)</td><td>3B; state-of-the-art in visual document retrieval; ColBERT-style late embedding interaction with the MaxSim retrieval similarity metric</td></tr>
        <tr><td>VLM</td><td>SmolVLM-Instruct</td><td>2.2B</td></tr>
        <tr><td>VLM</td><td>Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Instruct</td><td>3.75B</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
      <p>We split each dataset into a training set (80%, used to optimize the malicious image) and a test set (20%). For the above models, we assume the retriever only retrieves the top-1 relevant image from the KB.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Evaluation Metrics</h3>
    <div class="prose"><p>We evaluate the RAG system and the attack using the following performance metrics:</p></div>
    <div class="cards" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="card"><h4>Recall-B</h4><p>Recall-B is the fraction of queries for which <i>R</i> retrieves a relevant image prior to attack.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Recall-A</h4><p>Recall-A is the fraction of queries for which <i>R</i> retrieves a relevant image after the attack.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>ASR-R</h4><p>ASR-R is the fraction of queries for which the malicious image is retrieved by <i>R</i>.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="cards ops-join" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="card"><h4>ASR-G-Hard</h4><p>ASR-G-Hard is the fraction of queries for which the VLM outputs the target response <i>a</i>* verbatim, assuming that the malicious image is retrieved.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>ASR-G-Soft</h4><p>ASR-G-Soft is the average embedding similarity between the VLM generated response and the target response <i>a</i>*, assuming that the malicious image is retrieved, computed using the Jina-Embeddings-V3 text embedding model.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="results">
    <h2>Results</h2>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b class="red">1.000</b><span>ASR-R with CLIP-L on ViDoRe-V1-AI, for both VLMs</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>0.000</b><span>ASR-R against ColPali-v1.3 on ViDoRe-V1-AI</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>0.818</b><span>lowest ASR-G-Hard across all twelve combinations</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>8/255</b><span>α from which attacks almost perfectly satisfy both conditions</span></div>
    </div>

    <h3>Effect of Perturbation Intensity</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>We observe that attacks can almost perfectly satisfy both the retrieval and the generation conditions starting from α = 8/255. Therefore, in the rest of the paper, we consider only attacks with α = 8/255. Despite the success of the attack, the perturbed image is almost indistinguishable from the original.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Comparison of Different Models</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>As the previous results indicate, we observe high values of ASR-R, ASR-G-Hard, and ASR-G-Soft for most of the considered models, except ColPali-v1.3 which is almost unaffected by our attack. Increasing the allowed perturbation to α = 128/255 and training over 1500 gradient steps did not produce better results against ColPali-v1.3. Otherwise, CLIP-ViT-LARGE and Jina-CLIP-v2 almost always retrieve our malicious image.</p>
    </div>

