    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:24px">
      <p>Regarding generation, our attack consistently causes SmolVLM-Instruct and Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Instruct to generate the target answer verbatim for almost all user queries in the test dataset. We also observe an interesting interaction where Jina-CLIP-v2 seems to somewhat inhibit the learning of effective attacks against generation. In general, the results show that our attacks are successful against a broad range of models.</p>
    </div>
    <blockquote class="excerpt"><p>The image and textual embeddings produced by CLIP-ViT-LARGE andJina-CLIP-v2 are easily separable (and thus easier to attack), while those computed by ColPali-v1.3 are harder to separate.</p></blockquote>

    <h3>Attack Transferability</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>While the above evaluations assumed white-box access to the attacker, we also evaluated our attacks in the black-box setting, where the adversary does not have access to the embedding model and VLM used in the RAG system. We did not observe any transferability of the attack across the different embedding and VLM models. Attacks trained against a certain combination (embedding model, VLM) only work against the same combination. Therefore, exploring attacks that are trained simultaneously against multiple models is a direction for future research.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="limitations">
    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>We do not consider defenses to our proposed attack; however, we plan to evaluate our attack against a variety of defenses including re-rankers, multimodal LLM-as-a-Judge, and poisoned sample detectors in future work. Additionally, we aim to examine whether adaptive attacks could be crafted against specific defenses.</li>
        <li>Our attacks can be detected by filtering images that are retrieved too frequently; however, our attack uncovers a fundamental weakness of M-RAG pipelines that could be useful for hardening through adversarial training.</li>
        <li>Our evaluation is not comprehensive, as we do not consider a large number of embedding models, VLMs, datasets, metrics, and transferability evaluations. In future work, we aim to provide an extensive evaluation of the attack showing the generalization of the results observed in this paper.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p class="footnote">Transactions on Machine Learning Research, 2026: <a href="/papers/onepic/">One Pic is All it Takes: Poisoning Visual Document Retrieval Augmented Generation with a Single Image</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="conclusion">
    <h2>Conclusion</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>In this paper, we demonstrated the first poisoning attack against VD-RAG systems. Our attack showcased that conventional embedding models and VLMs are vulnerable to adversarial perturbation, where a single injected image is capable of causing a DoS of the entire RAG pipeline (both retrieval and generation). We also observed notable adversarial robustness of the ColPali-v1.3 embedding model and provided hints into why this is the case. Nevertheless, our attack provides the first step towards understanding the vulnerability of multimodal RAG systems and enhancing their robustness for future adoption.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="takeaways">
      <li><span>We propose the first poisoning attack against VD-RAG systems.</span></li>
      <li><span>We demonstrate that our multi-objective optimization approach allows an adversary to craft a single image that causes DoS against the entire RAG pipeline.</span></li>
      <li><span>We investigate several factors of attack success, such as the embedding model employed, the LMM, and the stealthiness of the injected image.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Paper>

